                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "sponge_holding_forceps_subsection_2",
          "subsection_title": "1. Introduction and identification",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "sponge_holding_forceps_group_2",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Sponge-holding forceps are surgical instruments designed to hold gauze swabs, cotton balls or surgical sponges during procedures. They are also used for gentle tissue handling, painting the operative site with antiseptic solution and absorbing blood or other fluids."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The commonly encountered ring-handled pattern is the Foerster sponge forceps, also known as sponge-holding forceps or sponge sticks."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "OSCE identification card"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Name: Sponge-holding forceps (Foerster pattern)"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Classification: Grasping and holding instrument"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Primary function: Holding swabs or sponges."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Characteristic feature: Long shafts terminating in oval or rounded, often fenestrated jaws, commonly with serrations."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Typical mechanism: Ring handles, a box joint and a ratchet lock."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Exam tip: If you are shown a long instrument with ring handles and rounded, loop-like jaws designed to hold a gauze swab, consider sponge-holding forceps."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "sponge_holding_forceps_subsection_3",
          "subsection_title": "2. Parts and labelling",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "sponge_holding_forceps_group_3",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/sponge_holding_forceps_02.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Part",
                    "Identification and function"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Ring handles",
                      "Provide grip and control during manipulation."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Ratchet lock",
                      "Holds the jaws closed at a selected setting."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Box joint",
                      "Joins the two halves and permits movement."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Shanks",
                      "Connect the handles to the working end."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Jaws",
                      "Hold the gauze, sponge or swab."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Oval or rounded tips",
                      "Provide a broad area for holding a swab."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Fenestrations",
                      "Openings in the jaws that help accommodate and retain the swab."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Serrated surfaces",
                      "Improve grip on the swab; the exact pattern varies by instrument."
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Not all sponge-holding forceps have identical jaw shapes or fenestrations. Always describe the actual instrument presented in the station."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "sponge_holding_forceps_subsection_4",
          "subsection_title": "3. Classification and types",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "sponge_holding_forceps_group_4",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Sponge-holding forceps may be classified according to jaw design, length and intended application."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/sponge_holding_forceps_03.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A. Straight sponge-holding forceps"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The working end lies along the axis of the shaft. Commonly used for swabbing, skin preparation and handling gauze in accessible operative fields."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/sponge_holding_forceps_04.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "B. Curved sponge-holding forceps"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The curved working end can improve access to selected anatomical areas and may help the operator reach around structures."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/sponge_holding_forceps_05.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "C. Long-pattern sponge forceps"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Used when the operative field is deeper or when greater reach is needed. Exact lengths vary by manufacturer and specialty."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The Foerster pattern is a widely recognized design. Other sponge-holding instruments may have different handles, jaw shapes or locking mechanisms."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "sponge_holding_forceps_subsection_5",
          "subsection_title": "4. Uses and clinical applications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "sponge_holding_forceps_group_5",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The main purpose is to hold a sponge securely while the surgeon or assistant uses it to clean, prepare or manage the operative field."
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Use",
                    "Explanation"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Skin preparation",
                      "Holds a swab soaked in an appropriate antiseptic solution to prepare the surgical site."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Swabbing",
                      "Absorbs blood and fluid from accessible operative surfaces."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Mopping",
                      "Helps clear blood or fluid to improve visibility."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Gentle tissue handling",
                      "May hold selected tissues when the instrument and technique are appropriate."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Blunt dissection",
                      "Can assist in limited situations with swab-assisted dissection, depending on the procedure."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Cavity preparation",
                      "Used to apply solutions or swab accessible cavities under appropriate clinical protocols."
                    ]
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "sponge_holding_forceps_group_6",
              "group_title": "Surgeries and procedures where they are used",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "General surgery: Skin preparation and swabbing during laparotomy, appendicectomy and other open procedures.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Obstetrics and gynaecology: Preparation and swabbing during caesarean section and selected vaginal or pelvic procedures.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Orthopaedic surgery: Swabbing and preparation of the operative site.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Urological surgery: Preparation and swabbing where appropriate.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Wound care: Holding gauze for cleaning and dressing procedures.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Important distinction: Sponge-holding forceps are primarily used to hold swabs, not to clamp blood vessels. They are also not the preferred instrument for grasping delicate bowel when a purpose-designed atraumatic tissue grasper is required."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "sponge_holding_forceps_subsection_6",
          "subsection_title": "5. Advantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "sponge_holding_forceps_group_7",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Secure swab handling: The jaws help prevent the swab from slipping during use.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Improved reach: The long shaft allows access to areas that are difficult to reach by hand.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Reduced direct contact: Allows the operator to handle antiseptic-soaked swabs without direct hand contact.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Improved operative visibility: Assists in clearing blood and fluid from the operative field.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Controlled application: Enables targeted application of antiseptic or other appropriate solutions.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Locking mechanism: On ratcheted models, the swab can be held without continuous squeezing.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "6."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Reusable designs: Stainless-steel models can be reprocessed for reuse according to validated protocols.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "7."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "sponge_holding_forceps_subsection_7",
          "subsection_title": "6. Disadvantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "sponge_holding_forceps_group_8",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Excessive pressure may damage tissue if the jaws are applied directly to it.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A poorly secured or inadequately loaded swab may slip off and be retained in the operative field.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Swabs can become saturated, reducing their effectiveness at absorbing fluid.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Long shafts may reduce tactile feedback and precision.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The jaws and hinge require careful cleaning because blood and organic material may remain in difficult-to-access areas.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A locking mechanism can make accidental over-compression possible.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "6."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The instrument is not designed to provide reliable haemostasis.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "7."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "sponge_holding_forceps_subsection_8",
          "subsection_title": "7. Complications and risks of improper use",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "sponge_holding_forceps_group_9",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Complication",
                    "How it occurs",
                    "Prevention"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Tissue trauma",
                      "Excessive pressure or rough swabbing",
                      "Use gentle pressure and an appropriate instrument."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Bleeding",
                      "Abrasion or injury to fragile tissue",
                      "Avoid forceful rubbing or traction."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Swab retention",
                      "A swab slips off or is not accounted for",
                      "Secure the swab and follow surgical swab-counting protocols."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Infection",
                      "Contaminated swabs, instruments or solutions",
                      "Maintain aseptic technique and appropriate reprocessing."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Chemical injury",
                      "Incorrect antiseptic use or pooling of solution",
                      "Follow the product instructions and procedure-specific precautions."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Foreign-body injury",
                      "Loose gauze fibres or a detached swab remain in the field",
                      "Inspect the swab and instrument; account for swabs before closure."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Accidental puncture or injury",
                      "Inappropriate use of the instrument near vulnerable structures",
                      "Maintain controlled movements and adequate visibility."
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "High-yield point: Swab retention is an important preventable surgical safety risk. Sponge-holding forceps do not replace formal swab counts, cavity checks or other required retained-item prevention measures."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "sponge_holding_forceps_subsection_9",
          "subsection_title": "8. Principles of proper use",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "sponge_holding_forceps_group_10",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/sponge_holding_forceps_01.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 1 — Inspect the instrument"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Confirm that the jaws align properly, the ratchet works smoothly and the instrument is intact and appropriately sterile."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/sponge_holding_forceps_06.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 2 — Secure the swab"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Place a suitable swab between the jaws and close them sufficiently to prevent slipping. Do not allow excessive amounts of loose gauze to hang from the jaws."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/sponge_holding_forceps_07.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 3 — Perform the intended task"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Use controlled movements for skin preparation, swabbing or fluid absorption. Follow the appropriate antiseptic protocol when preparing skin."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/sponge_holding_forceps_08.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 4 — Remove and account for the swab"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Release the instrument carefully, dispose of or process the swab appropriately and maintain the required surgical swab count."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "sponge_holding_forceps_subsection_10",
          "subsection_title": "9. Maintenance, cleaning and sterilization",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "sponge_holding_forceps_group_11",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "For reusable sponge-holding forceps:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Remove visible soil promptly in accordance with the facility's reprocessing protocol.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Pay particular attention to the box joint, ratchet and jaw serrations.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Check the jaws for alignment and the hinge for stiffness or damage.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Ensure that the ratchet locks and releases appropriately.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Use only manufacturer-approved cleaning agents and lubricants.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Sterilize using a validated process compatible with the instrument's instructions.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Do not assume that an instrument is sterile simply because it is made of stainless steel or has been cleaned."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "sponge_holding_forceps_subsection_11",
          "subsection_title": "10. Important OSCE comparisons",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "sponge_holding_forceps_group_12",
              "group_title": "Sponge-holding forceps versus Babcock forceps",
              "group_subtitle": null,
